-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261122.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-22)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The package owns every folder it puts a file into: /etc/zsh and /etc/update-motd.d are in its data again, empty,
    as they were up to 20261102 with the files in them. On a system without zsh-common the console was the only owner
    of /etc/zsh; 20261122 no longer shipped a file there, so dpkg removed the folder at the unpack and the postinst
    stopped with "cp: cannot create regular file '/etc/zsh/zshrc': No such file or directory" (the package
    half-configured, apt exit 100; measured on an installed machine and on a minimal edition system). A fresh install
    without zsh-common stopped the same way. A machine left half-configured by 20261122 configures with this version,
    a person's change to /etc/zsh/zshrc kept.
  * No empty folder of ours is left: /var/lib/sparky-stereo-console goes after the upgrade that empties it, the
    folders of the files ucf kept go at the removal when no other package lists them, and the folders under
    /var/backups/sparky-stereo-console go at the purge when nothing of the person's is in them.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261122~sparky9~0 (2026-11-22)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A person's change to the console's files under /etc is kept across upgrades (Debian Policy 10.7.3; row 813).
    Up to 20261121.1 all eight were plain files, and dpkg replaced them at every upgrade with no word.
  * /etc/motd, /etc/skel/.bash_aliases and /etc/skel/.selected_editor are conffiles.
  * /etc/issue, /etc/issue.net, /etc/update-motd.d/10-uname, /etc/nanorc and /etc/zsh/zshrc, at names the package
    diverts, are kept through ucf by the postinst from /usr/share/sparky-stereo-console/defaults (Depends: ucf): an
    untouched one follows the new default, a changed one stays and the new default is put beside it as FILE.ucf-dist
    and said once; ucf's records let dpkg-stereo --verify name a person's change to them. ucf's own question is
    answered by its default ("keep the local version") through debconf's noninteractive frontend, so nothing is
    asked; UCF_FORCE_CONFFOLD would keep an untouched file from the new default, and dpkg's --force-confdef
    overrides it (measured with ucf 3.0056). A conffile at a diverted name took the owning package's hash at
    install (dpkg asked about a file nobody had touched) and could not be removed (dpkg-divert refused to rename
    over it); measured with dpkg 1.23.11.
  * At the upgrade from an older version, an untouched file follows the new one and a changed one stays, the new
    default beside it, said once in ten languages; nothing is asked. At the removal, a changed file at a diverted
    name is kept in /var/backups/sparky-stereo-console and said, and the owning package's own file is back.
  * What a new account and a login show is unchanged.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261121.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-21)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Built with Lean Code 0.1.31, which reads /etc/skel/.bash_aliases as a bash file: it ships without its
    comments, as the skel .bashrc did up to 20261102, and the authored text is in sparky-stereo-console-lc-hr
    again, so no machine keeps an orphaned older companion.
  * The comment in /etc/skel/.bash_aliases says what its lines do; the words of Daniel it carried are in the
    recipe's comment, where they were up to 20261102.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261121~sparky9~0 (2026-11-21)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Rows 629/802: bash's /etc/skel/.bashrc is no longer diverted. The coloured prompt (red for root), the
    colour aliases, GCC's colours and nano as EDITOR and VISUAL come from /etc/skel/.bash_aliases, which
    Debian's skel .bashrc reads; a new account gets the same prompt, aliases and variables as before. The
    postinst gives bash's own .bashrc back at its name, with a person's change to it kept. A person's
    change to our former copy at /etc/skel/.bashrc is kept as /var/backups/sparky-stereo-console-skel.bashrc
    (not in /etc/skel, which useradd copies whole into each new account) and said, in the ten languages, on
    the terminal and in the journal.
  * The diversions that stay (issue, issue.net, 10-uname, nanorc, zshrc: their owners offer no place of
    their own) no longer cost the owning package its conffile: when the owner was unpacked earlier in the
    same dpkg run, the preinst moves its pending PATH.dpkg-new to the diverted name before our file is
    unpacked (nano's /etc/nanorc was never installed on fresh installs, dpkg recording it "newconffile").
    The postrm does the reverse and removes only diversions that are this package's.

